  the two trivial cycles are; the only sign-specific fact is the gate 2^(a+c) vs 9, i.e. the sign law.

==============================================================================
S6  (vi) word-function invariants are blind; the order-only residue-of-minimum law
==============================================================================
PROVED (word-function theorem): if an invariant I(n) is a function of (b, parity word of n), then the
  number of residues mod 2^J with I in any set is the same on both sheets after b -> -b (control S2 bijection).
  Hence a sign-specific invariant must use the ORDER of Z (positivity or size), not residues or words alone.

residue-of-minimum law (order + residue): for a T_b cycle with minimum n_0 > 1 the step out of n_0 ascends,
  (3n_0+b)/2^k > n_0 forces k = 1, so 3n_0+b = 2 mod 4: plus n_0 = 3 mod 4, minus n_0 = 1 mod 4;
  the maximum M > 1 descends, forcing k >= 2, i.e. 3M+b = 0 mod 4: plus M = 1 mod 4, minus M = 3 mod 4.
  sheet -1 cycle (5, 7): min 5 = 1 mod 4 (k=1), max 7 = 3 mod 4 (k=2)
  sheet -1 cycle (17, 25, 37, 55, 41, 61, 91): min 17 = 1 mod 4 (k=1), max 91 = 3 mod 4 (k=4)
  the plus fixed point 1 has k = 2 (3+1 = 4), the minus fixed point 1 has k = 1 (3-1 = 2): the root asymmetry again.
  Decisive test (plus sheet, order-only): the last odd value > 1 before a T_+ orbit hits 1 is a descending
  (k >= 2) node, hence 1 mod 4 by the law; indeed it is (4^j - 1)/3 = 1 + 4 + ... + 4^(j-1) = 1 mod 4 for the
  j with 3n+1 = 4^j.  The census over odd 3 <= n <= 10^6 confirms it and shows the law has no exclusion content:
  last odd value before 1, residue mod 4 histogram over odd 3 <= n <= 10^6: [(1, 499999)]
  every plus orbit to 10^6 ends through a 1 mod 4 node (exactly the maximum-type residue), as the law says;
  a hypothetical plus cycle would also obey it with its own min = 3 mod 4 and max = 1 mod 4.

conjugation check: the law for the minus sheet is the law for negative n on the plus sheet (T_+(-n) = -T_-(n)),
  and -1 mod 4 = 3 mod 4: the residue-of-minimum law is the sign law read mod 4.  It is SIGN-SPECIFIC in its
  statement and reduces to the sign law; it is not new.

SURVIVING SIGN-SPECIFIC CONTENT (this lane's conclusion): positivity of n on the chosen sheet, equivalently
  sign(B_L) = b, equivalently which side of log_2 3 a cycle's K/L must lie on; plus the finite verification
  floor (2^68 plus, 10^7 minus, cited).  Every candidate (i)-(vi) is either a word function (blind), a
  conjugation-image of the sign law, or a finite fact.  The ONE root asymmetry is S1: at n = 1 and only there
  the two sheets' odd arrows fail strictness differently (halved: diagonal (1,1) vs companion 0;
  unhalved: strict 1+3 vs diagonal 1+1), and 1 is fixed by the plus companion map (n+1)/2, killed by (n-1)/2.

